      "parts": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\"The RNNT speech‑recognition example relies on the librosa library for audio preprocessing steps such as mel‑filterbank generation, resampling, silence trimming, and short‑time Fourier transform (STFT). Recent versions of librosa have changed the signatures of several functions: the mel filterbank generator now requires keyword arguments, the resample function expects explicit `orig_sr` and `target_sr` parameters, the trim utility uses a `top_db` keyword instead of a positional argument, and the STFT routine returns a complex tensor by default rather than a real‑valued magnitude array. Because the existing code still calls these functions with the old positional argument style and assumes the previous return format, executing the RNNT pipeline results in runtime errors or incorrect feature tensors, causing the model inference to fail or produce invalid results.\n\nThe required behavior is that the preprocessing pipeline should correctly invoke the updated librosa functions using the appropriate keyword arguments, handle the complex output of the STFT by converting it to a real‑valued representation, and then compute the power spectrum as before. After these adjustments, the pipeline must be able to load audio files, optionally resample them, optionally trim silence, compute mel filterbanks, and produce a tensor of acoustic features that can be fed into the RNNT model without raising exceptions. The overall functionality of the RNNT example – downloading data, preparing features, and running inference – should work unchanged from the user’s perspective, but now be compatible with the newer librosa release.\n\nImplement these updates in the audio feature extraction and segment handling components of the RNNT example, ensuring that all calls to librosa use the new keyword‑argument conventions and that any necessary conversion of complex STFT output to real values is performed before further processing. The solution should preserve the original numerical behavior of the feature extraction pipeline while eliminating the compatibility issues introduced by the library upgrade.\"\n",
          "id": "prt_eff7ccd3f001ZQ3PU63UzemJcz",
          "sessionID": "ses_1008332d9ffeAV8BeAl4UDnfoP",
          "messageID": "msg_eff7ccd3e0014ml5EVKH1k21IA"
        }
      ]
